The B runtime was not in unix-jun72 itself. I found it in Angelo Papenhoff’s reconstructed B environment, under aap/b/unix1_bdir:

obrt1.s — older B startup/runtime
obilib/ — older B interpreter library
olibb/ — older B library

Its README explicitly identifies those as older runtime/library variants. Papenhoff’s TUHS announcement says the regular runtimes were restored from s2 tape binaries, with older variants also reconstructed, and that reversed B programs could be rebuilt to exact matches apart from assembler differences.

The strongest match was obrt1.s: it contains the same Init\n diagnostic found inside /bin/su, constructs the B argv area, then transfers control into threaded B code. That established that much of su should be interpreted using the old B runtime rather than as a normal linear PDP-11 assembly program.

