USING THE RECOVERED /BIN/SU
===========================

The recovered First Edition /bin/su uses the syntax documented by the
First Edition manual:

        su password

It does not prompt interactively. The password must be supplied as the
first command-line argument.


PASSWORD
--------

For the 746-byte /bin/su binary included in this repository, the
password consists of four control characters:

        octal bytes:    021 022 023 024
        ASCII names:    DC1 DC2 DC3 DC4
        keyboard:       CTRL-Q CTRL-R CTRL-S CTRL-T

From a normal-user shell, type:

        su <CTRL-Q><CTRL-R><CTRL-S><CTRL-T><RETURN>

The four control characters are not printed on screen.

With the supplied VT05/telnet setup, they can be entered directly. Some
other terminal programs may interpret CTRL-Q and CTRL-S as XON/XOFF
flow-control characters rather than passing them to UNIX.

An incorrect password, or invoking su without the argument, prints:

        Sorry


SET-USER-ID MODE
----------------

Authentication alone is not sufficient. /bin/su must be owned by root
and have its set-user-ID bit.

The original s2-bits tape inventory records:

        urwr-  0  746  ...  /bin/su

However, a generated unix-jun72 image may contain it as an ordinary
executable:

        xrwr-  0  746  ...  /bin/su

In that state the password is accepted and su executes another shell,
but the shell still runs under the original user ID. The prompt remains
'@' instead of changing to the root '#' prompt.

As root, restore the original mode with:

        chmod 76 /bin/su

The listing should then start with:

        surwr-

After this correction, invoking su with the four control characters
produces a root shell with the '#' prompt.


VERIFICATION
------------

The following behaviour was confirmed on the unix-jun72 reconstruction:

        su
                prints "Sorry"

        su with an incorrect argument
                prints "Sorry"

        su with bytes 021 022 023 024, without set-user-ID mode
                does not print "Sorry"
                starts a shell with the ordinary-user '@' prompt

        chmod 76 /bin/su
        su with bytes 021 022 023 024
                starts a root shell with the '#' prompt

This distinguishes the password check from the separate set-user-ID
permission problem.


BINARY ANALYSIS
---------------

The exact repository binary was exported and examined:

        pathname:   /bin/su
        size:       746 bytes
        a.out type: 0405
        SHA-256:
        c5a3331c357bd5ab9307c848d0e08d7926ba465db08e42d23c1a6b852851f20f

Visible strings include:

        /bin/sh
        Sorry
        Init
        LV out of range

The executable contains a small early B program linked with an old PDP-11
B runtime. It is therefore not correctly understood as one continuous
ordinary PDP-11 assembly program: part of it is threaded B code dispatched
through runtime routines.

The password data decodes to:

        021 022 023 024 000

The final zero is the string terminator.


B RUNTIME CONTEXT
-----------------

The runtime model used for analysis came from Angelo Papenhoff's
reconstruction of the early UNIX B environment:

        repository: aap/b
        directory:  unix1_bdir
        runtime:    obrt1.s
        library:    obilib/
        B library:  olibb/

The unix1_bdir README describes obrt1, obilib and olibb as older versions
of the reconstructed B runtime and libraries.

The obrt1 startup code contains the same "Init" diagnostic found in su.
It constructs the B argument vector and then jumps into threaded B code,
which explains the structure of the recovered executable.

Papenhoff reports that the reconstructed B tools and runtimes were
derived from recovered object files, libraries and binaries from the s2
tape, and were used to rebuild reverse-engineered B programs with exact
matches apart from assembler differences.

The original B source code for this particular su has not been recovered.
The password and behaviour described here come from examination of the
preserved executable followed by testing on the running reconstruction.


OPTIONAL RAW-BYTE LAUNCHER
--------------------------

If a terminal program consumes CTRL-Q or CTRL-S instead of transmitting
them, the password can be supplied without passing through terminal input.

Create su0.s:

        sys exec; path; args
        sys exit

        path: </bin/su\0>
        pass: 011021;012023;0
        args: path;pass;0

Assemble and run:

        as su0.s
        a.out

The two octal words store the four password bytes in PDP-11 little-endian
order. This launcher is only a fallback; direct keyboard entry works with
the supplied VT05 setup.

The tape inventory confirms that the preserved /bin/su is 746 bytes and originally had mode urwr-, so documenting or correcting the lost set-user-ID permission is appropriate for the reconstruction repository.

Suggested pull-request description
This PR documents how to use the recovered First Edition /bin/su.

The preserved binary expects the four control bytes 021, 022, 023 and
024 as its password argument. These can be entered directly as
CTRL-Q, CTRL-R, CTRL-S and CTRL-T in the supplied VT05 terminal setup.

The original s2-bits inventory records /bin/su with mode urwr-, but the
built image I tested contained it without the set-user-ID bit. In that
state authentication succeeds, but su launches another ordinary-user
shell. Restoring the historical mode with `chmod 76 /bin/su` makes it
produce the expected root shell.

The note also records the binary analysis and the relationship to the
reconstructed early B runtime in aap/b/unix1_bdir.

This PR is documentation-only. A separate build change may be appropriate
to preserve the original set-user-ID mode automatically.
